  "rationale": "All listed candidates are direct matches to atrial fibrillation by trait_reported/trait_efo, but many have endpoint packaging penalties (e.g., 5-year predicted risk models PGS004612/PGS004613, CHARGE-AF-adjusted covariates in PGS005168, and various lifestyle/exposure interaction contexts) or are framework/portability-style scores (e.g., UKB 813-traits snpnet PGS001340; portability study scores without comparable PRS-only metrics). Among disease-focused candidates with clear AF endpoints, PGS005168 (Nat Genet 2025 meta-analysis for atrial fibrillation) has the strongest visible outcome discrimination signal (C-index 0.8718 in EUR validation, n=52,757) and comes from a large multi-cohort AF study context, making it the best-supported direct-match choice in this context. Limitation: its reported performance is a full-model metric with CHARGE-AF included among covariates, which the provided domain guidance flags as risk-wrapper packaging and makes the headline C-index less PRS-comparable; many other candidates also lack explicit PRS-only metrics, so certainty about standalone PRS superiority versus other disease-focused genome-wide scores (e.g., PGS000016/PGS000331) remains limited."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target concept (nodular goiter), including (i) benign nodular goiter (PGS005261/PGS005262/PGS005263) and (ii) nontoxic multinodular goiter (PGS001814/PGS002022). Models contrasting thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter (PGS005273/PGS005274) are less endpoint-faithful to a generic nodular goiter target because they are case-vs-case discrimination rather than disease risk for nodular goiter.

Among the most phenotype-aligned candidates, PGS005262 is preferred because it targets “Benign nodular goiter” directly and, within its publication family, has the strongest visible performance signal (highest reported full-model AUROC 0.5933 vs 0.5854 and 0.5876; and the largest reported effect size OR 1.408 per SD vs 1.048 and 1.362). This provides better support than the UKB portability-framework multinodular goiter scores (PGS001814/PGS002022), which only report weak partial correlations (~0.024–0.028) and come from a portability study archetype.

Limitation: for PGS005262 the covariates are listed as “Unknown,” and the AUROC shown is a full-model metric (not explicitly PRS-only), so comparability of the reported discrimination to standalone PRS is uncertain; additionally, there remains some endpoint ambiguity between ‘benign nodular goiter’ vs ‘nontoxic multinodular goiter’ for the broad target “nodular goiter,” preventing a high-confidence designation."}

