  "rationale": "Both candidates are direct matches to the target trait (trait_efo = nicotine dependence; phenotyping_reported/trait_reported = Tobacco use disorder) and come from the same UKB portability publication with identical training cohort (UKB), training n (391,124), covariates, and validation sample size (19,400), so endpoint fidelity and study context do not differentiate them. The clearest visible differentiator is PRS-comparable performance: PGS002037 shows a higher selected European partial correlation (partial-r 0.0741, CI 0.0601–0.0881) than PGS001830 (0.0557, CI 0.0416–0.0697), and it also has a much larger variant count (847,691 vs 13,838), which is a supportive structural signal within this comparable setting. Confidence is moderated because both are from the same portability framework (a known weaker archetype than disease-focused studies in general) and neither reports PRS-only AUC/R2, limiting comparability beyond partial-r."
